#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719563 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b9b11c6-36dd-5e3d-8a14-7d72431a3381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719570} {:answer/id #uuid "d3c036bf-fe96-5954-89b2-bdd5ceaaca06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719575} {:answer/id #uuid "dc193cbd-1e56-5b7d-a180-16e6c17340b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719576} {:answer/id #uuid "ef03653d-43dc-5acd-a737-431dbba10a81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719580} {:answer/id #uuid "f72e1479-8ee6-51c4-a58e-c4973790e27c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719581}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719580} :answer-field/id #uuid "76bba247-a820-516d-b9cd-6c08abbbe522" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "30300ab3-f7a7-504b-b83e-ad714a5fcd22" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x^2+y.$ The curve $C$ is parametrized by\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 2t,t^2+1\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\leq t\\leq 1,\n$$\nwith orientation given by increasing $t.$ Evaluate\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The potential $f$ has a continuous gradient everywhere, and $\\mathbf r$ is smooth. The fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=f(\\mathbf r(1))-f(\\mathbf r(0)).\n$$\nThe initial and terminal points are\n$$\n\\mathbf r(0)=\\langle 0,1\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf r(1)=\\langle 2,2\\rangle.\n$$\nEvaluate the potential at both endpoints, then subtract the initial value:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n&=f(2,2)-f(0,1)\\\\\n&=(2^2+2)-(0^2+1)\\\\\n&=6-1\\\\\n&=5.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719565 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a2c10c7-fd21-5d40-9ef1-6897a7509dc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719562} {:answer/id #uuid "9a3c690b-bdc6-514b-a68d-39addb0a9c84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719572} {:answer/id #uuid "bb547e39-ca66-5d83-8228-72c645fbba70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719573} {:answer/id #uuid "dca72a8f-37b0-5706-8ab8-63a51d8586f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719577} {:answer/id #uuid "e4212c29-c0f6-58ba-9dd2-6bb614c63daf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719578}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719573} :answer-field/id #uuid "7af77cf2-e48f-51e0-ad9b-a2aaaabede6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "52b6f33f-7941-5629-b586-50fa86fc2554" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=x\\sin y+y\\cos z.$ The curve $C$ is parametrized by\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle\\sin t,t,2t\\rangle,\\qquad \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\leq t\\leq\\pi,\n$$\nwith orientation given by increasing $t.$ Evaluate\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The potential has a continuous gradient everywhere and the curve is smooth, so the fundamental theorem for line integrals applies:\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n=f(\\mathbf r(\\pi))-f\\left(\\mathbf r\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\right).\n$$\nThe endpoints are\n$$\n\\mathbf r\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)=\\left\\langle 1,\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\pi\\right\\rangle,\n\\qquad \\mathbf r(\\pi)=\\langle 0,\\pi,2\\pi\\rangle.\n$$\nTheir potential values are\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf\\left(1,\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\pi\\right)&=1\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\cos\\pi=1-\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\\\\nf(0,\\pi,2\\pi)&=0\\sin\\pi+\\pi\\cos(2\\pi)=\\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTherefore,\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n=\\pi-\\left(1-\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719574 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "202b8f84-9ca5-5d34-b0c6-d4a48f265d3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{15}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719561} {:answer/id #uuid "42cf042d-27dd-5220-b2f7-60dc127c5137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719564} {:answer/id #uuid "85433ea2-a855-5b6f-bd89-8828eff18037" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719569} {:answer/id #uuid "8f077bb1-eeb8-543e-9c47-f00691c06bc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719571} {:answer/id #uuid "eafd2b9a-0e4b-5654-83f7-4bd68131ca13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719579}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719571} :answer-field/id #uuid "592945e4-a623-513a-a1ee-7d71a472da7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "cf728f38-58e0-5982-91b2-9a5b3c8057ae" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=xy+z$ and let $b>1.$ The curve $C$ is parametrized by\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,t,t^2+1\\rangle,\\qquad 1\\leq t\\leq b,\n$$\nwith orientation given by increasing $t.$ If\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=16,\n$$\nwhat is $b$?" :question/worked-solution "The potential has a continuous gradient everywhere and the curve is smooth. By the fundamental theorem for line integrals,\n$$\n16=f(\\mathbf r(b))-f(\\mathbf r(1)).\n$$\nThe initial point is $\\mathbf r(1)=\\langle 1,1,2\\rangle,$ and the terminal point is $\\mathbf r(b)=\\langle b,b,b^2+1\\rangle.$ Thus,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(\\mathbf r(1))&=1\\cdot 1+2=3,\\\\\nf(\\mathbf r(b))&=b\\cdot b+(b^2+1)=2b^2+1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSubstitute these values into the endpoint difference and solve:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n16&=(2b^2+1)-3\\\\\n16&=2b^2-2\\\\\nb^2&=9.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $b>1,$ the required value is $b=3.$ Substituting it back gives $f(3,3,10)-f(1,1,2)=19-3=16.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719660 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "053a9000-20c3-5a53-bcd1-d29cc3d80404" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719658} {:answer/id #uuid "1093bdd5-2727-5bfd-9ce6-a5a9e8b691ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719659} {:answer/id #uuid "5fe0540b-bff2-5585-ba7c-cbdef96be3f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719668} {:answer/id #uuid "8371f512-561f-5e46-aa7f-e0d0e73545ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719671} {:answer/id #uuid "b31add32-04f2-5a72-932a-242bc83c102c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719675}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719671} :answer-field/id #uuid "48c24e6c-1ef1-506f-a05f-f57dc1c42562" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1e0335ae-a54d-5a92-858b-19404573a414" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, traversed in the direction of increasing $t$, where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle 1+\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right),1+t\\right\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nEvaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\nfor $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(2xy+y)\\,\\mathbf i+(x^2+x-2y)\\,\\mathbf j$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "We seek $f$ with $f_x=2xy+y$ and $f_y=x^2+x-2y$. Integrating $f_x$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x^2y+xy+g(y).\n$$\n\nNow match its $y$ derivative to the second component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^2+x+g'(y)&=x^2+x-2y,\\\\\ng'(y)&=-2y,\\\\\ng(y)&=-y^2+K.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoose $K=0$, giving $f(x,y)=x^2y+xy-y^2$. Its gradient is exactly $\\mathbf F$.\n\nThe endpoints are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r(0)&=\\langle 1,1\\rangle,\\\\\n\\mathbf r(1)&=\\langle 2,2\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe field is continuous, so the fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n&=f(2,2)-f(1,1)\\\\\n&=(8+4-4)-(1+1-1)\\\\\n&=8-1\\\\\n&=7.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe term $-y^2$ is essential: integrating only the first component does not determine the whole potential."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719674 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "258c78d1-c5c6-57ba-b7c8-2dcc8f8691a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719661} {:answer/id #uuid "400f1d91-9244-51a7-8a66-645835647a84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719663} {:answer/id #uuid "48b7dc17-94f2-511d-af35-6ba662998d17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719664} {:answer/id #uuid "64686ea5-fbf5-5b81-8508-794e5bdc3bca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719669} {:answer/id #uuid "9b52ded9-afa2-5cd1-8a4e-aea65ef529a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719672}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719661} :answer-field/id #uuid "abd1742b-615b-5e29-b2db-36e54e26021d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "affffd6a-fcf5-58ef-8925-38f7d5544909" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, traversed in the direction of increasing $t$, where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 1+t,2+t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nEvaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\nfor $\\mathbf F(x,y)=y\\,\\mathbf i+x\\,\\mathbf j$.\n\n*Hint: $\\mathbf F$ is conservative. Find a potential function, then subtract its value at the starting point from its value at the ending point.*" :question/worked-solution "A potential function $f$ must satisfy $f_x=y$ and $f_y=x$. Integrating the first equation with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=xy+g(y).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $y$ and matching the second component gives $x+g'(y)=x$, so $g$ is constant. We can choose\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=xy.\n$$\n\nThe curve starts at $\\mathbf r(0)=\\langle 1,2\\rangle$ and ends at $\\mathbf r(1)=\\langle 2,3\\rangle$. Since $\\nabla f=\\mathbf F$ is continuous along $C$, the fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n&=f(2,3)-f(1,2)\\\\\n&=2\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 2\\\\\n&=4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe arbitrary constant in a potential cancels in this difference."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719678 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28f66eee-7687-54fb-9faf-b5b0aca3ddcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "44+(\\ln 3)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719662} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd2facf-9724-5eee-ac49-4158da1e706a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}+(\\ln 3)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719667} {:answer/id #uuid "7c8506e9-45e7-5bdb-824e-72c0a85d4362" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}-(\\ln 3)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719670} {:answer/id #uuid "d206808f-ac1e-5b5d-8d57-53155c46ce5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719676} {:answer/id #uuid "e2535b80-ecf4-531e-a3ee-71c914078b70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}+(\\ln 3)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719677}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719667} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b863642-0389-5c6a-b990-f9acbefe0fd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e5fd31df-7c37-589d-8f1d-2c81afb27636" :question/problem "Consider the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=2xe^{-y}\\,\\mathbf i+(2y-x^2e^{-y})\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the curve $y=\\ln(x-1)$ for $2\\le x\\le 4$, traversed in the direction of increasing $x$." :question/worked-solution "Write $P=2xe^{-y}$ and $Q=2y-x^2e^{-y}$. These functions have continuous partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$, and\n\n$$\nP_y=Q_x=-2xe^{-y}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbb R^2$ is simply connected, $\\mathbf F$ is conservative. To construct a potential, integrate $f_x=P$ with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x^2e^{-y}+g(y).\n$$\n\nMatching $f_y$ to $Q$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x^2e^{-y}+g'(y)&=2y-x^2e^{-y},\\\\\ng'(y)&=2y,\\\\\ng(y)&=y^2+K.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe may choose $K=0$, so $f(x,y)=x^2e^{-y}+y^2$. Differentiation verifies both components of $\\mathbf F$.\n\nA parametrization consistent with the orientation is $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,\\ln(t-1)\\rangle$ for $2\\le t\\le 4$. The curve is smooth on this interval, starts at $(2,0)$, and ends at $(4,\\ln 3)$.\n\nApply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, using $e^{-\\ln 3}=1/3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n&=f(4,\\ln 3)-f(2,0)\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{16}{3}+(\\ln 3)^2\\right)-4\\\\\n&=\\frac{4}{3}+(\\ln 3)^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly the oriented endpoints enter this final evaluation; differentiating the logarithmic path and integrating the resulting dot product is unnecessary."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719855 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "515eb200-4374-5c4b-b77d-e3ae892373a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719860} {:answer/id #uuid "5fdbaf17-108d-5906-886c-e7dfc77459b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719862} {:answer/id #uuid "690009bb-5985-5eb5-8563-cc9d15adb540" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719864} {:answer/id #uuid "83345b07-2744-5299-b2d6-41edd5013476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719867} {:answer/id #uuid "8e9bcbb8-6307-5bf1-9187-5b1a2dc3d4f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719868}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719867} :answer-field/id #uuid "eee04163-cc98-5bb9-af15-4783219ed543" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "03310b0e-88aa-5b79-b61a-50bbcc202101" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=xy+y^2.$ Let $C$ be the part of the curve $y^2=x+1$ lying below the $x$-axis, traversed from its intersection with $x=3$ to its intersection with $x=0.$ Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The path lies below the $x$-axis, so $y$ must be negative. At $x=3,$ the curve equation gives $y^2=4,$ hence $y=-2.$ At $x=0,$ it gives $y^2=1,$ hence $y=-1.$ In the stated direction, the initial and terminal points are therefore $A=(3,-2)$ and $B=(0,-1).$\n\nTo describe this directed arc, set $y=t$ and write $x=t^2-1.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t^2-1,t\\rangle,\\qquad -2\\le t\\le-1.\n$$\n\nAs $t$ increases, $x$ decreases from $3$ to $0,$ agreeing with the prescribed traversal. The gradient is continuous everywhere. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r\n&=f(B)-f(A)\\\\\n&=f(0,-1)-f(3,-2)\\\\\n&=\\bigl(0\\cdot(-1)+(-1)^2\\bigr)-\\bigl(3\\cdot(-2)+(-2)^2\\bigr)\\\\\n&=1-(-2)\\\\\n&=3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the positive values of $y$ would select a different arc. Reversing the order of the two endpoint values would change the sign of the integral."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719869 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "216b2159-db6a-5096-ac1b-02a711262f4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719857} {:answer/id #uuid "3157bee7-88bb-5ae5-acfc-d23bfea402e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719859} {:answer/id #uuid "63cff1f7-9c72-5381-9824-b98b498ed90f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719863} {:answer/id #uuid "6a1bdfb4-7f00-5614-a73a-ae467ec36bd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719865} {:answer/id #uuid "7d432ff3-8bc1-5db6-b196-f928bb96c3a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719866}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719866} :answer-field/id #uuid "e59fb5df-26b8-5664-9045-6c069b3e204b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c4d37012-1f74-5a0e-aee8-a63d2433c941" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x^2+y^2.$ Let $C$ follow the curve $\\dfrac{x}{2}=\\sqrt{y}$ as $y$ increases from $1$ to $4.$ Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The interval is specified in terms of $y.$ Set $y=t$ and rearrange the curve equation to obtain $x=2\\sqrt{t}.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 2\\sqrt{t},t\\rangle,\\qquad 1\\le t\\le4.\n$$\n\nThus the initial point is $A=\\mathbf r(1)=(2,1)$ and the terminal point is $B=\\mathbf r(4)=(4,4).$ The gradient is continuous everywhere. Applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r\n&=f(B)-f(A)\\\\\n&=f(4,4)-f(2,1)\\\\\n&=(4^2+4^2)-(2^2+1^2)\\\\\n&=32-5\\\\\n&=27.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719875 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1191d889-4002-5f55-85c7-6fec2ed7c35f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719856} {:answer/id #uuid "3070a8b7-4707-5e83-87bf-65cb2f1a2dd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719858} {:answer/id #uuid "51ae0ba4-cfe1-5494-875a-419724030fdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719861} {:answer/id #uuid "d0bdc870-3d21-566c-a098-4ec3b154bcb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719870} {:answer/id #uuid "fa0a1a3d-32e5-59d9-b121-ebf6e1b6fbe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719874}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719858} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddb7ddf8-d6c4-5a95-95b5-0951db69f03a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fb34da0f-4969-5e10-b89c-82ba4087cbc2" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=xy.$ Let $C$ follow the curve $y=x+1$ as $x$ increases from $0$ to $2.$ Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Use $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,t+1\\rangle$ for $0\\le t\\le 2.$*" :question/worked-solution "The supplied parametrization gives the initial point $A=\\mathbf r(0)=(0,1)$ and terminal point $B=\\mathbf r(2)=(2,3).$ Since $f$ is differentiable and $\\nabla f$ is continuous everywhere, the fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r\n&=f(B)-f(A)\\\\\n&=f(2,3)-f(0,1)\\\\\n&=2\\cdot3-0\\cdot1\\\\\n&=6.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719944 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7174b8ff-d035-5df8-bfb5-b1b1118193a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719948} {:answer/id #uuid "7af47906-1b57-5ad9-8cd1-b5c6894b2aad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719950} {:answer/id #uuid "919e39b1-d834-5efa-8eaf-2bee3bb8af85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719954} {:answer/id #uuid "bd6cb26d-5987-5b6b-bb47-d3ae6864328d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719960} {:answer/id #uuid "d198d259-064e-5288-a4d0-e765c0b38571" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719963}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719963} :answer-field/id #uuid "3364dffd-0d52-5710-add4-fcfe5ae638c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "46707ef5-1ecc-530d-aeab-1714578e3cc2" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=- 2 x^{2} - 2 y.$ The curve $C$ is parametrized by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle 3 t,t^{2} + 1\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTraverse $C$ from $t=0$ to $t=1.$ Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The fundamental theorem for line integrals gives the terminal potential minus the initial potential. The endpoints are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r(0)&=\\left\\langle 0,1\\right\\rangle, \\\\ \\mathbf r(1)&=\\left\\langle 3,2\\right\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute these points into the supplied potential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(0,1)&=- 2 \\left(0\\right)^{2} - 2 \\left(1\\right) \\\\ &=-2, \\\\ f(3,2)&=- 2 \\left(3\\right)^{2} - 2 \\left(2\\right) \\\\ &=-22.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=f(3,2)-f(0,1) \\\\ &=\\left(-22\\right)-\\left(-2\\right) \\\\ &=-20.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719949 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63e573f5-7183-5948-b5d6-ad467e63a40d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{12}{e^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719947} {:answer/id #uuid "ac1e9261-f9eb-5d2b-8549-a0c5f6c2d5cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2 + \\frac{2}{e^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719958} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b92ad0-b947-5bf8-b648-3d0b6f4bbb62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2 - \\frac{10}{e^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719964} {:answer/id #uuid "ea420608-c6be-5e10-95e9-0c39b80a9156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{6}{e^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719967} {:answer/id #uuid "ecd18df2-4831-5f71-8685-43b73825a957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719969}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719967} :answer-field/id #uuid "df64fc77-5d0f-5c97-bae8-1207cbc2d002" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "798f35ef-f87e-50aa-acd8-446583e8aa25" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=2 y e^{2 x}.$ The curve $C$ is parametrized by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t,- 3 t^{2}\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTraverse $C$ from $t=0$ to $t=-1.$ Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The fundamental theorem for line integrals gives the terminal potential minus the initial potential. The endpoints are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r(0)&=\\left\\langle 0,0\\right\\rangle, \\\\ \\mathbf r(-1)&=\\left\\langle -1,-3\\right\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute these points into the supplied potential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(0,0)&=2 \\left(0\\right) e^{2 \\left(0\\right)} \\\\ &=0, \\\\ f(-1,-3)&=2 \\left(-3\\right) e^{2 \\left(-1\\right)} \\\\ &=- \\frac{6}{e^{2}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=f(-1,-3)-f(0,0) \\\\ &=\\left(- \\frac{6}{e^{2}}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\ &=- \\frac{6}{e^{2}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719956 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "383746b1-4e31-5b9a-93a8-9e77f50111be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 4 \\pi + 4 \\pi^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719943} {:answer/id #uuid "823d4232-5807-5fac-8310-b9819d7c6c33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 8 \\pi + 4 \\pi^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719951} {:answer/id #uuid "bd6c2229-23bd-54d7-9d4b-c1ba5396f2ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 16 \\pi + 4 \\pi^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719959} {:answer/id #uuid "f3a6ca34-f960-5e58-b079-f051d3b334b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8 + 8 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719972} {:answer/id #uuid "fa4707d0-b548-54a7-b7c8-0c1b676f7f4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 6 \\pi^{2} - 8 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719974}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719951} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce765170-0a9c-5285-bdf1-763d9b001bd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a55f19bd-c8ae-5e57-a334-fdf11d649acf" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} y - 2 x y^{2}.$ The curve $C$ is parametrized by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t - \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},1 - \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTraverse $C$ from $t=2 \\pi$ to $t=\\pi.$ Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The fundamental theorem for line integrals gives the terminal potential minus the initial potential. The endpoints are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r(2 \\pi)&=\\left\\langle 2 \\pi,0\\right\\rangle, \\\\ \\mathbf r(\\pi)&=\\left\\langle \\pi,2\\right\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute these points into the supplied potential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(2 \\pi,0)&=2 \\left(2 \\pi\\right)^{2} \\left(0\\right) - 2 \\left(2 \\pi\\right) \\left(0\\right)^{2} \\\\ &=0, \\\\ f(\\pi,2)&=2 \\left(\\pi\\right)^{2} \\left(2\\right) - 2 \\left(\\pi\\right) \\left(2\\right)^{2} \\\\ &=- 8 \\pi + 4 \\pi^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=f(\\pi,2)-f(2 \\pi,0) \\\\ &=\\left(- 8 \\pi + 4 \\pi^{2}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\ &=- 8 \\pi + 4 \\pi^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719957 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83b3c91b-18c4-57ac-a8cf-c951024aab8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719952} {:answer/id #uuid "83eba62b-6598-5552-a91c-14b99771aae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719953} {:answer/id #uuid "9c0bc3c0-ff28-5130-b758-168ca84e2354" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719955} {:answer/id #uuid "e0c70483-af72-5900-ac7f-06f73c523e8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719966} {:answer/id #uuid "eb83efe8-eadc-5a89-99a7-a2fd0295d00d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719968}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719966} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9724eb8-45b6-52ec-8daf-9c3cd5223b5b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "aa3c8c4e-e129-5a68-b2b0-18d5272e79e8" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=- x^{3} - y.$ The curve $C$ is parametrized by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle 3 t,t^{3}\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTraverse $C$ from $t=-1$ to $t=1.$ Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The fundamental theorem for line integrals gives the terminal potential minus the initial potential. The endpoints are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r(-1)&=\\left\\langle -3,-1\\right\\rangle, \\\\ \\mathbf r(1)&=\\left\\langle 3,1\\right\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute these points into the supplied potential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-3,-1)&=- \\left(-3\\right)^{3} - \\left(-1\\right) \\\\ &=28, \\\\ f(3,1)&=- \\left(3\\right)^{3} - \\left(1\\right) \\\\ &=-28.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=f(3,1)-f(-3,-1) \\\\ &=\\left(-28\\right)-\\left(28\\right) \\\\ &=-56.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719970 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18c4f947-42ba-551e-8ac4-68fa8b20ea7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48 e^{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719940} {:answer/id #uuid "204615df-0927-50c6-8c86-394feed024aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719941} {:answer/id #uuid "471d0891-d21d-5310-a275-2b62a70dc6f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719945} {:answer/id #uuid "4beeea03-43b3-5902-a3c4-47c5ed459804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36 e^{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719946} {:answer/id #uuid "eed876f3-ae4f-5759-9de4-ce4edd40b95e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719971}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719941} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1795876-3597-5261-a568-d3a6b6e0f905" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719961}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ee8eb08d-a3e3-5c1d-9487-8d9901d919a1" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x^{2} y e^{x y}.$ The curve $C$ is parametrized by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTraverse $C$ from $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$ Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The fundamental theorem for line integrals gives the terminal potential minus the initial potential. The endpoints are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r(0)&=\\left\\langle 3,0\\right\\rangle, \\\\ \\mathbf r\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)&=\\left\\langle 0,4\\right\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute these points into the supplied potential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(3,0)&=\\left(3\\right)^{2} \\left(0\\right) e^{\\left(3\\right) \\left(0\\right)} \\\\ &=0, \\\\ f(0,4)&=\\left(0\\right)^{2} \\left(4\\right) e^{\\left(0\\right) \\left(4\\right)} \\\\ &=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=f(0,4)-f(3,0) \\\\ &=\\left(0\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\ &=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720079 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29ef7d4b-c5ae-500b-9372-70d92b42af9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\pi}{2} + \\frac{5 \\pi^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720065} {:answer/id #uuid "9f9c830c-b6e1-549d-a7bb-cef5eeeb012e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{3 \\pi}{2} + \\frac{3 \\pi^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720080} {:answer/id #uuid "ae528d8b-d9aa-55f7-b650-15a3dfc45f88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\pi + \\pi^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720083} {:answer/id #uuid "bc349fcc-25cd-5b58-b4bd-16e7a40015db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720084} {:answer/id #uuid "d6c38021-af56-5643-acd0-9a50d991b86a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 3 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720091}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720080} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2ad0f58-afa0-52d1-a2ee-81d230364d53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720095}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8e831c68-0470-5faf-b7f5-3c0de17dbc07" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y^{2} + y$. Let $C$ follow the curve $x=\\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - 1$ as $y$ goes from $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ to $- \\pi$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Set $y=t$ and use the Cartesian equation to obtain the other coordinate. A parametrization in the stated direction is $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 1,t\\right\\rangle$, with $t$ going from $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ to $- \\pi$. The initial point is $A=\\left(-1,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ and the terminal point is $B=\\left(-2,- \\pi\\right)$. The gradient is continuous on this arc, so the fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(B)&=\\left(- \\pi\\right)^{2} + \\left(- \\pi\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\pi + \\pi^{2}\\\\\nf(A)&=\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^{2} + \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{\\pi}{2} + \\frac{\\pi^{2}}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting $I=\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=f(B)-f(A)\\\\\n&=\\left(- \\pi + \\pi^{2}\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad -\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} + \\frac{\\pi^{2}}{4}\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{3 \\pi}{2} + \\frac{3 \\pi^{2}}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720081 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f93178e-f8d7-52bf-966c-48222a50f568" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720070} {:answer/id #uuid "49818755-ec6d-51f4-aafd-4fdab6fdc16c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720071} {:answer/id #uuid "5285c720-feae-585f-bb3c-ecaace1bcaba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- e^{\\pi} + \\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720072} {:answer/id #uuid "a36923ba-6bc7-5cd2-8bf8-eb138b2336c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720082} {:answer/id #uuid "eb16b4f5-bfe1-5e73-afdd-44331dea2cee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\pi}{2} + e^{\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720093}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720093} :answer-field/id #uuid "344ece65-b716-5168-a419-cf627335d3c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a0e9515a-c377-5a11-bf83-0182c8185f2a" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x - y + e^{y} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}$. Let $C$ follow the curve $y=2x$ as $y$ goes from $0$ to $\\pi$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Set $y=t$ and use the Cartesian equation to obtain the other coordinate. A parametrization in the stated direction is $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle \\frac{t}{2},t\\right\\rangle$, with $t$ going from $0$ to $\\pi$. The initial point is $A=\\left(0,0\\right)$ and the terminal point is $B=\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\pi\\right)$. The gradient is continuous on this arc, so the fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(B)&=\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) - \\left(\\pi\\right) + e^{\\left(\\pi\\right)} \\sin{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\right)}\\\\\n&=- \\frac{\\pi}{2} + e^{\\pi}\\\\\nf(A)&=\\left(0\\right) - \\left(0\\right) + e^{\\left(0\\right)} \\sin{\\left(\\left(0\\right) \\right)}\\\\\n&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting $I=\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=f(B)-f(A)\\\\\n&=\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{2} + e^{\\pi}\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad -\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{\\pi}{2} + e^{\\pi}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720085 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a9ef184-8aa4-5a84-9294-ceda1997e24d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720074} {:answer/id #uuid "7e81cf76-d702-5b88-b69e-fc4b519709d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720075} {:answer/id #uuid "c35a9a73-7bd9-505a-ab7a-8f233e049127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720086} {:answer/id #uuid "f0c8d847-08df-507c-8bb2-9d47ceaec4a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720094} {:answer/id #uuid "fc22019a-18d4-56b0-8990-18f256b777bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720096}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720074} :answer-field/id #uuid "36e9ab12-22ef-5afc-a73e-35645d61e5d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c24be72e-09c0-57d1-a2bf-8fc1191942f0" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=- x^{2} + y^{2} + y$. Let $C$ follow the curve $y^2=4\\left(x - 1\\right)$ as $y$ goes from $0$ to $2$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Set $y=t$ and use the Cartesian equation to obtain the other coordinate. A parametrization in the stated direction is $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle \\frac{t^{2}}{4} + 1,t\\right\\rangle$, with $t$ going from $0$ to $2$. The initial point is $A=\\left(1,0\\right)$ and the terminal point is $B=\\left(2,2\\right)$. The gradient is continuous on this arc, so the fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(B)&=- \\left(2\\right)^{2} + \\left(2\\right)^{2} + \\left(2\\right)\\\\\n&=2\\\\\nf(A)&=- \\left(1\\right)^{2} + \\left(0\\right)^{2} + \\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting $I=\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=f(B)-f(A)\\\\\n&=\\left(2\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad -\\left(-1\\right)\\\\\n&=3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720088 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "051e3558-cb4b-52c1-bdb3-2d15204eea2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720062} {:answer/id #uuid "0e6773ee-35d6-5b5b-b0f5-6b3f10efefcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720063} {:answer/id #uuid "2f6ff65b-d878-5886-ac29-5c3574acd837" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1 + \\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720067} {:answer/id #uuid "d5d5fa53-05d8-5dbd-b6ed-007f760e477c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2} + 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720090} {:answer/id #uuid "e1bf08e8-8972-5ebc-84b1-d3426368cf79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720092}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720062} :answer-field/id #uuid "60a5a9ef-ddc8-5bdd-a91a-40ebf85ab1e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ceaf0a2c-565f-50eb-86a8-82a1b007074f" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=- x - y + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}$. Let $C$ follow the curve $2x=\\pi y^3$ as $y$ goes from $-1$ to $0$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Set $y=t$ and use the Cartesian equation to obtain the other coordinate. A parametrization in the stated direction is $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle \\frac{\\pi t^{3}}{2},t\\right\\rangle$, with $t$ going from $-1$ to $0$. The initial point is $A=\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{2},-1\\right)$ and the terminal point is $B=\\left(0,0\\right)$. The gradient is continuous on this arc, so the fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(B)&=- \\left(0\\right) - \\left(0\\right) + \\cos{\\left(\\left(0\\right) \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\left(0\\right) \\right)}\\\\\n&=1\\\\\nf(A)&=- \\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) - \\left(-1\\right) + \\cos{\\left(\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\left(-1\\right) \\right)}\\\\\n&=1 + \\frac{\\pi}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting $I=\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=f(B)-f(A)\\\\\n&=\\left(1\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad -\\left(1 + \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{\\pi}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720089 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16159f97-0952-597c-8050-66c283e0297d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720064} {:answer/id #uuid "831766f5-4df8-5954-9777-2973fe848fb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- e^{12} - 10 + e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720076} {:answer/id #uuid "87c54cef-1c47-50d3-beb8-f514e2a0d4b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- e^{16} - 9 + e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720077} {:answer/id #uuid "88f06014-80b0-51f7-9b0a-527a4002a2a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- e + 3 + e^{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720078} {:answer/id #uuid "c94cecf9-ba82-5431-a9b2-4105b3a54428" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- e^{12} - 3 + e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720087}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720087} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b2010f3-9b46-5929-8ecb-dc19a44f5e55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d2bbe67a-d31e-56ca-8a92-6d4a267377ed" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x^{2} - x - y + e^{x y}$. Let $C$ follow the curve $\\frac{x + 1}{2}=\\sqrt{y}$ as $y$ goes from $4$ to $1$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Set $y=t$ and use the Cartesian equation to obtain the other coordinate. A parametrization in the stated direction is $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle 2 \\sqrt{t} - 1,t\\right\\rangle$, with $t$ going from $4$ to $1$. The initial point is $A=\\left(3,4\\right)$ and the terminal point is $B=\\left(1,1\\right)$. The gradient is continuous on this arc, so the fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(B)&=\\left(1\\right)^{2} - \\left(1\\right) - \\left(1\\right) + e^{\\left(1\\right) \\left(1\\right)}\\\\\n&=-1 + e\\\\\nf(A)&=\\left(3\\right)^{2} - \\left(3\\right) - \\left(4\\right) + e^{\\left(3\\right) \\left(4\\right)}\\\\\n&=2 + e^{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting $I=\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=f(B)-f(A)\\\\\n&=\\left(-1 + e\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad -\\left(2 + e^{12}\\right)\\\\\n&=- e^{12} - 3 + e.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720105 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6aaa07de-135d-5f2f-94fe-dcb79809b606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720107} {:answer/id #uuid "6f74ae60-30f1-5d8a-af40-1cb20f1ffc80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720109} {:answer/id #uuid "98244f2c-1cee-55f1-ad3c-070b08c7f5d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720112} {:answer/id #uuid "bbc26ea7-73ad-5ba3-8d0c-47eb87a82eab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720116} {:answer/id #uuid "bd76ceef-d014-51f7-8ff6-f352dcdd8121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720117}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720109} :answer-field/id #uuid "038f7bba-c80c-5c60-9418-1a739d3e8749" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5aca61d5-b2b3-5070-83f0-82ce589b7d89" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=2 x y - 2 y^{2}$. Let $C$ be the part of the curve $y^2=x-1$ lying below the $x$-axis, traversed from its intersection with $x=10$ to its intersection with $x=2$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The specified half-plane selects the negative values of $y$. Solving the curve equation at the two boundary lines gives the directed endpoints below. A parametrization in the stated direction is $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t^{2} + 1,t\\right\\rangle$, with $t$ going from $-3$ to $-1$. The initial point is $A=\\left(10,-3\\right)$ and the terminal point is $B=\\left(2,-1\\right)$. The gradient is continuous on this arc, so the fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(B)&=2 \\left(2\\right) \\left(-1\\right) - 2 \\left(-1\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&=-6\\\\\nf(A)&=2 \\left(10\\right) \\left(-3\\right) - 2 \\left(-3\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&=-78\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting $I=\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=f(B)-f(A)\\\\\n&=\\left(-6\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad -\\left(-78\\right)\\\\\n&=72.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720110 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f1fb309-a8ba-54d8-b941-9c540b317b8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720103} {:answer/id #uuid "29f33cd1-53d5-55b7-b369-a949781c52b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720104} {:answer/id #uuid "6eca7b8f-4662-5ab5-a016-2dd35eb8d58f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720108} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d844b2-ff95-5dca-878a-7d705feca978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720115} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b0e4dc-96d4-5cbb-8cde-cfe4bc712442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720124}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720115} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4c262fe-96ce-5c67-a958-eb37dbc5d60c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7c0c0ade-fc4a-57ca-b50e-6c1f6c26b7e4" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=- x^{2} + 2 x y$. Let $C$ be the part of the curve $x^2=y-1$ lying to the left of the $y$-axis, traversed from its intersection with $y=5$ to its intersection with $y=2$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The specified half-plane selects the negative values of $x$. Solving the curve equation at the two boundary lines gives the directed endpoints below. A parametrization in the stated direction is $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t,t^{2} + 1\\right\\rangle$, with $t$ going from $-2$ to $-1$. The initial point is $A=\\left(-2,5\\right)$ and the terminal point is $B=\\left(-1,2\\right)$. The gradient is continuous on this arc, so the fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(B)&=- \\left(-1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(-1\\right) \\left(2\\right)\\\\\n&=-5\\\\\nf(A)&=- \\left(-2\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(-2\\right) \\left(5\\right)\\\\\n&=-24\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting $I=\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=f(B)-f(A)\\\\\n&=\\left(-5\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad -\\left(-24\\right)\\\\\n&=19.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720111 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14e88a8a-0a7c-571f-951f-3d8794213421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720101} {:answer/id #uuid "1af6258b-99b7-5a23-b7e4-8921ee1c5e79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720102} {:answer/id #uuid "c1e801ae-0c05-5fc8-9f5f-62a011f5b8e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720118} {:answer/id #uuid "d6dd3efa-c670-5ad3-996e-e61aa7a96f91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720120} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb2e6c1-a2cf-524a-baaa-f081d7f1cb28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720125}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720102} :answer-field/id #uuid "05da4861-6bfa-55ea-83c2-1d4973b32f31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "88ffeac7-30ae-5caa-a070-960449d0fef9" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=- 2 x y + 2 y^{2}$. Let $C$ be the part of the curve $y^2=x+2$ lying above the $x$-axis, traversed from its intersection with $x=-1$ to its intersection with $x=2$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The specified half-plane selects the positive values of $y$. Solving the curve equation at the two boundary lines gives the directed endpoints below. A parametrization in the stated direction is $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t^{2} - 2,t\\right\\rangle$, with $t$ going from $1$ to $2$. The initial point is $A=\\left(-1,1\\right)$ and the terminal point is $B=\\left(2,2\\right)$. The gradient is continuous on this arc, so the fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(B)&=- 2 \\left(2\\right) \\left(2\\right) + 2 \\left(2\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&=0\\\\\nf(A)&=- 2 \\left(-1\\right) \\left(1\\right) + 2 \\left(1\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&=4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting $I=\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=f(B)-f(A)\\\\\n&=\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad -\\left(4\\right)\\\\\n&=-4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720121 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06022a90-184f-5e7f-ac5d-5dc7022f37c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720100} {:answer/id #uuid "669c2f3c-3ef4-5ca7-a34c-0ec207c4e945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720106} {:answer/id #uuid "c57cbca8-ada4-5251-a5f2-3ed358a6b58c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720119} {:answer/id #uuid "e744748b-05d3-5ef6-8023-4f6ebbecfb69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-83" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720122} {:answer/id #uuid "f56c1819-2ddd-5bc6-83fe-03f03a2ee29c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720123}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720123} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b87fcec-a14c-5503-a509-3287454d94b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e675713f-7b1c-5d98-9532-05f04af8b549" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=- x^{2} + 2 x y$. Let $C$ be the part of the curve $x^2=y$ lying to the left of the $y$-axis, traversed from its intersection with $y=4$ to its intersection with $y=9$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The specified half-plane selects the negative values of $x$. Solving the curve equation at the two boundary lines gives the directed endpoints below. A parametrization in the stated direction is $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t,t^{2}\\right\\rangle$, with $t$ going from $-2$ to $-3$. The initial point is $A=\\left(-2,4\\right)$ and the terminal point is $B=\\left(-3,9\\right)$. The gradient is continuous on this arc, so the fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(B)&=- \\left(-3\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(-3\\right) \\left(9\\right)\\\\\n&=-63\\\\\nf(A)&=- \\left(-2\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(-2\\right) \\left(4\\right)\\\\\n&=-20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting $I=\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=f(B)-f(A)\\\\\n&=\\left(-63\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad -\\left(-20\\right)\\\\\n&=-43.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720126 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c914405-f243-5256-9bf0-4f0e8ed2a98c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720131} {:answer/id #uuid "702e017c-75a6-56bb-8bb5-70713b335ac7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720132} {:answer/id #uuid "b2aac205-3ae7-53f1-bac7-9fdae3f9411a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720133} {:answer/id #uuid "ce308edc-d972-58b6-9095-68ace20657bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720134} {:answer/id #uuid "d461cab8-3e75-5be5-b069-028faaadec2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720136}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720133} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed3dd1a5-3fdf-57fa-a188-faecc173873b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "08290163-91ea-5970-9edf-d1d02cfa1fcd" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 1,1,0\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 0,1,2\\rangle$." :question/worked-solution "First compute the cross product using a determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b}\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}&\\mathbf{j}&\\mathbf{k}\\\\1&1&0\\\\0&1&2\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=(1\\cdot2-0\\cdot1)\\mathbf{i}\\\\\n&\\quad-(1\\cdot2-0\\cdot0)\\mathbf{j}\\\\\n&\\quad+(1\\cdot1-1\\cdot0)\\mathbf{k}\\\\\n&=2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}=\\langle 2,-2,1\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe magnitude of this cross product is the parallelogram's area:\n\n$$\nA=\\|\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b}\\|=\\sqrt{2^2+(-2)^2+1^2}=\\sqrt{9}=3.\n$$\n\nThe lesson diagram illustrates this relationship; its arrows show general vectors.\n\n![The cross-product magnitude equals the area of the parallelogram spanned by two vectors.](images/27/2717ed1209f60dac12c3fcb07aea25f99c49b053fe0ce440b7ce996002a067c7.png)\n\nWe do not halve the magnitude here because the requested shape is a parallelogram."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720130 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11673e30-a0b9-5ffd-9069-542019b13f51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720127}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720127} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe107254-4dad-5fbd-b15b-bbcf55b09d80" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720138}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5243c5e2-4ab1-570c-8789-c9f32d4852a1" :question/problem "The triangle spanned by $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle x,1,0\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 0,1,2\\rangle$ has area $\\sqrt{6}$. Given that $x>0$, find $x$.\n\n$x=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Compute the cross product in terms of the unknown component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b}\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}&\\mathbf{j}&\\mathbf{k}\\\\x&1&0\\\\0&1&2\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=(1\\cdot2-0\\cdot1)\\mathbf{i}\\\\\n&\\quad-(x\\cdot2-0\\cdot0)\\mathbf{j}\\\\\n&\\quad+(x\\cdot1-1\\cdot0)\\mathbf{k}\\\\\n&=2\\mathbf{i}-2x\\mathbf{j}+x\\mathbf{k}=\\langle 2,-2x,x\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe magnitude is the area of the parallelogram, which is twice the given triangle area. Thus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac12\\|\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b}\\|&=\\sqrt{6}\\\\\n\\frac12\\sqrt{2^2+(-2x)^2+x^2}&=\\sqrt{6}\\\\\n\\sqrt{4+5x^2}&=2\\sqrt{6}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth sides are nonnegative. Squaring and solving gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4+5x^2&=24\\\\\n5x^2&=20\\\\\nx^2&=4\\\\\nx&=\\pm2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x>0$, the required value is $x=2$. Checking this in the original area formula gives\n\n$$\n\\frac12\\sqrt{4+5(2)^2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{24}}2=\\sqrt{6},\n$$\n\nwhich agrees with the given triangle area."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720135 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b210b8a-a304-5973-ad22-4d33e0383419" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720129}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720129} :answer-field/id #uuid "12ffd2f3-c553-5050-8d3a-34874ea88be3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d335a093-0964-54ba-96ea-7472d0392a67" :question/problem "Calculate the area $A_{\\triangle}$ of the triangle spanned by $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 1,-1,2\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 2,1,0\\rangle$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$A_{\\triangle}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Compute the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b}\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}&\\mathbf{j}&\\mathbf{k}\\\\1&-1&2\\\\2&1&0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=((-1)\\cdot0-2\\cdot1)\\mathbf{i}\\\\\n&\\quad-(1\\cdot0-2\\cdot2)\\mathbf{j}\\\\\n&\\quad+(1\\cdot1-(-1)\\cdot2)\\mathbf{k}\\\\\n&=-2\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}=\\langle -2,4,3\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding parallelogram has area\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{\\mathrm{parallelogram}}&=\\|\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b}\\|\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{(-2)^2+4^2+3^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{29}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle is half of that parallelogram, so\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle}=\\frac12\\|\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b}\\|=\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720227 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40b5cf2d-d9b2-562e-b284-6997d66c0d83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720230} {:answer/id #uuid "60005fde-e756-517f-8599-a79038091e43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{35}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720234} {:answer/id #uuid "89255cb9-6873-56f6-ad4a-7868efa37566" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720239} {:answer/id #uuid "b72c64ea-58fd-56b8-8856-627e9f374a9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{34}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720243} {:answer/id #uuid "c28f23c9-93bd-5372-bdff-91bf1059b6d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720244}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720234} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6a89202-d3ba-5d93-a53c-86d28593b8f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "005f0b0f-f5fd-53df-87cd-0da5c3a9125a" :question/problem "Calculate the area of triangle $PQR$ with vertices $P(1,-1,0)$, $Q(2,1,-1)$, and $R(0,0,2)$. Give the area in square units." :question/worked-solution "Form two side vectors with the same starting point:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{PQ}&=\\langle2-1,1-(-1),-1-0\\rangle=\\langle1,2,-1\\rangle,\\\\\n\\overrightarrow{PR}&=\\langle0-1,0-(-1),2-0\\rangle=\\langle-1,1,2\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTake their cross product:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{PQ}\\times\\overrightarrow{PR}\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}&\\mathbf{j}&\\mathbf{k}\\\\1&2&-1\\\\-1&1&2\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=(2\\cdot2-(-1)\\cdot1)\\mathbf{i}\\\\\n&\\quad -(1\\cdot2-(-1)\\cdot(-1))\\mathbf{j}\\\\\n&\\quad +(1\\cdot1-2\\cdot(-1))\\mathbf{k}\\\\\n&=5\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}=\\langle5,-1,3\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe parallelogram's area is the magnitude of this vector:\n$$\n\\lVert\\overrightarrow{PQ}\\times\\overrightarrow{PR}\\rVert=\\sqrt{5^2+(-1)^2+3^2}=\\sqrt{35}.\n$$\nThe triangle occupies half the parallelogram, so\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\sqrt{35}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720229 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75fd01ab-a982-55c9-a401-f94566466515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac12\\lVert(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a})\\times(\\mathbf{c}+\\mathbf{a})\\rVert" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720237} {:answer/id #uuid "80d64180-5ebe-5561-9283-cc2ef887beab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lVert(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a})\\times(\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a})\\rVert" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720238} {:answer/id #uuid "8d48a5aa-6fa9-508f-8c42-34bc3af385a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac12\\lVert(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a})\\times(\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a})\\rVert" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720240} {:answer/id #uuid "d0a4e558-170c-55ba-8e9a-ae3d2a22a028" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac12\\left|(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a})\\cdot(\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a})\\right|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720246} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e4b41a-bc52-5b09-ae81-4592d2ebb20c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac12\\lVert\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c}\\rVert" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720249}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720240} :answer-field/id #uuid "68608939-4f4c-5910-9a8d-4f11f7846142" :answer-field/key "formula" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720235} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51f39406-4d4f-5306-9b3f-3b0c3d92a262" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720231}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720231} :answer-field/id #uuid "a702ed0e-7146-5789-ba48-7ca733bbb026" :answer-field/key "area" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "381fcaee-4aab-515f-9be4-666d4e125353" :question/problem "Triangle $ABC$ has vertices $A(1,1,1)$, $B(2,-1,3)$, and $C(3,2,0)$. Let $\\mathbf{a},\\mathbf{b},\\mathbf{c}$ be their position vectors from the origin.\n\nCalculate the triangle's exact area in square units.\n\n$T=$ {{area}}\n\nAlso choose the expression below that gives this triangle's area." :question/worked-solution "Position vectors start at the origin. To get sides of this triangle, subtract the same vertex from the other two:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}&=\\langle2-1,-1-1,3-1\\rangle=\\langle1,-2,2\\rangle,\\\\\n\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a}&=\\langle3-1,2-1,0-1\\rangle=\\langle2,1,-1\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThus the correct expression is\n$$\nT=\\frac12\\lVert(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a})\\times(\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a})\\rVert.\n$$\nUsing $\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c}$ would give an area based at the origin, not at $A$. A dot product does not give parallelogram area. The sum $\\mathbf{c}+\\mathbf{a}$ is not the side $\\overrightarrow{AC}$, and omitting the factor $\\frac12$ gives the parallelogram's area.\n\nNow compute:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a})\\times(\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a})\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}&\\mathbf{j}&\\mathbf{k}\\\\1&-2&2\\\\2&1&-1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=((-2)(-1)-2\\cdot1)\\mathbf{i}\\\\\n&\\quad -(1\\cdot(-1)-2\\cdot2)\\mathbf{j}\\\\\n&\\quad +(1\\cdot1-(-2)\\cdot2)\\mathbf{k}\\\\\n&=0\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k}=\\langle0,5,5\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nIts magnitude is $\\sqrt{0^2+5^2+5^2}=\\sqrt{50}=5\\sqrt2$. Therefore,\n$$\nT=\\frac{5\\sqrt2}{2}.\n$$\nReversing the cross-product order reverses its direction but leaves this area unchanged."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720232 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "250f129f-7dce-5925-a6fe-522b7a3e35c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720228} {:answer/id #uuid "727d2c03-922c-5c91-89e3-62aa6f701b1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720236} {:answer/id #uuid "abd3998f-553f-58da-9e79-5c8a2bda6deb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720242} {:answer/id #uuid "e61515c9-aa29-5b77-90eb-7a6d40a9685e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720247} {:answer/id #uuid "e7131387-b794-5624-a5f4-46a1ec6cd805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720248}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720228} :answer-field/id #uuid "5afc6aaf-f299-5447-a9d0-1778aa1d00c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "55929d3a-ae7f-5f76-aab3-f4a9cd82a9ce" :question/problem "Calculate the area of triangle $ABC$ with vertices $A(1,0,1)$, $B(3,0,1)$, and $C(1,1,2)$.\n\nUse the two sides starting at $A$:\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=B-A,\\qquad \\mathbf{v}=C-A,\\qquad T=\\frac12\\lVert\\mathbf{u}\\times\\mathbf{v}\\rVert.\n$$\nThe diagram shows the parallelogram spanned by these sides, with $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{v}$. The triangle has half its area.\n\n![](images/27/2717ed1209f60dac12c3fcb07aea25f99c49b053fe0ce440b7ce996002a067c7.png)\n\nChoose the triangle's area in square units." :question/worked-solution "First subtract the coordinates of the common vertex:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{AB}&=\\langle3-1,0-0,1-1\\rangle=\\langle2,0,0\\rangle,\\\\\n\\overrightarrow{AC}&=\\langle1-1,1-0,2-1\\rangle=\\langle0,1,1\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nCompute the cross product component by component:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{AB}\\times\\overrightarrow{AC}\n&=\\langle0\\cdot1-0\\cdot1,\\;0\\cdot0-2\\cdot1,\\;2\\cdot1-0\\cdot0\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle0,-2,2\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nIts magnitude is the parallelogram's area:\n$$\n\\lVert\\overrightarrow{AB}\\times\\overrightarrow{AC}\\rVert=\\sqrt{0^2+(-2)^2+2^2}=\\sqrt8=2\\sqrt2.\n$$\nThe triangle's area is half this value:\n$$\nT=\\frac12(2\\sqrt2)=\\sqrt2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720258 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02676718-b242-5798-8438-f04e3a93e3f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 24 e^{6} - 3 e^{2} + 12 + 3 e^{3} + 3 e^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720251} {:answer/id #uuid "04f72173-26ca-510e-8b80-dcec46b1c59b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 24 e^{6} - 3 e^{2} + 6 + 3 e^{3} + 3 e^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720253} {:answer/id #uuid "77f968cc-1d3a-52e7-8ed1-dfdf8b5651de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 8 e^{6} - 3 e^{2} + 6 + e^{3} + 3 e^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720272} {:answer/id #uuid "bd11e576-a9e5-5071-925c-fd461c6bf11c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 36 e^{6} + 4 + 9 e^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720278} {:answer/id #uuid "d59c570d-b3da-5bf9-bca6-79f490e3a195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3 \\left(- 8 e^{4} - 1 + e + e^{2}\\right) e^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720280}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720253} :answer-field/id #uuid "537c43f2-c327-5e67-840a-fae8721f9ae4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "41965fbe-f232-5ebe-873a-6435346e3e8b" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, traversed in the direction of decreasing $t$, where\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t,e^{t}\\rangle,\\,t\\in [1,2].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(9 x^{2} y^{3} - 4 x\\right)\\,i+\\left(9 x^{3} y^{2} - 6 y\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Write $P=9 x^{2} y^{3} - 4 x$ and $Q=9 x^{3} y^{2} - 6 y.$ A potential must satisfy $f_x=P$ and $f_y=Q$. Integrating the first component with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=3 x^{3} y^{3} - 2 x^{2}+g(y)\n$$\n\nMatch its $y$ derivative to the second component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 x^{3} y^{2}+g'(y)&=9 x^{3} y^{2} - 6 y \\\\ g'(y)&=- 6 y \\\\ g(y)&=- 3 y^{2}+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoose $K=0$, since a constant cancels in the endpoint difference. Thus\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=3 x^{3} y^{3} - 2 x^{2} - 3 y^{2}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating verifies both components of the given field. For the stated orientation, the starting and ending points are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A&=(2,e^{2}) \\\\ B&=(1,e)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the potential at each endpoint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(B)&=3 \\left(1\\right)^{3} \\left(e\\right)^{3}- 2 \\left(1\\right)^{2}- 3 \\left(e\\right)^{2} \\\\ &=- 3 e^{2} - 2 + 3 e^{3} \\\\ f(A)&=3 \\left(2\\right)^{3} \\left(\\left(e^{2}\\right)\\right)^{3}- 2 \\left(2\\right)^{2}- 3 \\left(\\left(e^{2}\\right)\\right)^{2} \\\\ &=- 3 e^{4} - 8 + 24 e^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(B)-f(A) \\\\  & =\\left(- 3 e^{2} - 2 + 3 e^{3}\\right)-\\left(- 3 e^{4} - 8 + 24 e^{6}\\right) \\\\  & =- 24 e^{6} - 3 e^{2} + 6 + 3 e^{3} + 3 e^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
